/ActualText (The LSTM \(the AI's memory\) processes the 5-hour grid history and compresses it into a 64-dimensional hidden state \(a vector of 64 numbers\). This is the AI's "internal understanding" of the current grid situation. t-SNE is a mathematical technique that takes these complex 64-dimensional vectors and squashes them down to 2 dimensions \(X and Y coordinates\) so we can plot them on a graph.)
/K [3]
/S /Span
/Type /StructElem
/P 4716 0 R
/Pg 4048 0 R
>>
endobj
4716 0 obj
<<
/K [4715 0 R]
/S /P
/Type /StructElem
/P 4709 0 R
>>
endobj
4717 0 obj
<<
/ActualText (The AI looks at a 5-hour window of grid data \(hours t-4, t-3, t-2, t-1, and t\). But does it actually use all 5 hours equally? Or does it focus more on certain hours?Temporal Attribution \(using a technique called occlusion\) answers this by systematically "blinding" the AI to specific hours and seeing how much its decision changes.)
/K [4]
/S /Span
/Type /StructElem
/P 4718 0 R
/Pg 4048 0 R
>>
endobj
4718 0 obj
<<
/K [4717 0 R]
/S /P
/Type /StructElem
/P 4709 0 R
>>
endobj
4719 0 obj
<<
/ActualText (This is the most sophisticated test. We're asking: "Is the LSTM implicitly learning to predict the future, even though we never explicitly trained it to do so?" The LSTM is trained only to minimize carbon emissions, not to forecast. But if it's truly learning grid dynamics, its internal hidden state should contain information about what will happen in the next few hours.)
/K [5]
/S /Span
/Type /StructElem
/P 4720 0 R
/Pg 4048 0 R
>>
endobj
4720 0 obj
<<
/K [4719 0 R]
/S /P
/Type /StructElem
/P 4709 0 R
>>
endobj
4721 0 obj
<<
/ActualText (Every time the AI makes a decision, it outputs: 1.An action: "Schedule on CAISO". 2.A confidence score: The probability it assigned to that action \(e.g., 0.82 = 82% confident\). Later, we observe the actual reward \(negative carbon cost\) that resulted from that decision. The confidence-reward correlation measures: "When the AI is confident, does it actually get better outcomes?" We collect thousands of \(confidence, reward\) pairs. Then we calculate the Pearson correlation coefficient.)
/K [6]
/S /Span
/Type /StructElem
/P 4722 0 R
/Pg 4048 0 R
>>
endobj
